            "text": "{\n  \"primary_meaning\": \"God's covenant people (Israel) and the divine blessings of peace, prosperity, and spiritual fruitfulness associated with the Promised Land.\",\n  \"primary_basis\": [\n    \"Deuteronomy 8:8\",\n    \"Judges 9:8-13\",\n    \"Habakkuk 3:17\",\n    \"Joel 1:7-12\",\n    \"Micah 4:4\"\n  ],\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Distinct dispensational representations, where the Fig Tree represents national Israel, the Vine represents Israel's spiritual privileges (or the Church/Christ), and the Olive Tree represents Israel's covenantal and religious roots.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Hosea 9:10\",\n        \"Jeremiah 24:1-10\",\n        \"Matthew 24:32\",\n        \"John 15:1-5\",\n        \"Romans 11:17-24\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Eschatological timekeeping, specifically reading the 'budding of the fig tree' alongside the other plants as prophetic indicators of the end times and the modern restoration of the State of Israel.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Matthew 24:32-34\",\n        \"Luke 21:29-31\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"General spiritual fruitfulness, domestic peace, and divine provision applied individually to believers or the home.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Psalm 52:8\",\n        \"Psalm 128:3\",\n        \"John 15:5\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_settled_consensus\": true,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"notes\": \"As an agricultural triad, the olive, vine, and fig frequently appear together in the Old Testament to symbolize the health and wealth of the Promised Land. When God blesses Israel, these thrive; in judgment, they fail. While mainstream exegesis views them collectively as representing God's covenant blessings upon Israel, Dispensational interpreters have historically assigned specific, distinct symbolic identities to each plant.\"\n}",
